+  "title": "GOV-0121: DISCOVERED — the doctrine file is section-scoped by `## ` headings; 32 token assertions depend on heading depth and nothing tells an editor",
+  "problem": "🧠 **ARCHI (BARCHI-2)** — DISCOVERED while editing `GITHUB_CONTROL_PLANE.md` in PR #278. **Nobody's fault in that PR; filed separately per *Discovery ≠ scope expansion*.**\n\n**Id claim:** `GOV-0121`. Ceiling swept at time of filing: **`GOV-0120`** (main tree, issue titles+bodies, issue comments, all remote branch heads). Next free ≥ `GOV-0122`.\n\n## The hazard\n\n`docs/governance/GITHUB_CONTROL_PLANE.md` is edited constantly by governance seats writing prose. Two tests scope assertions to a *section*, delimited by the **next `## ` heading**:\n\n```python\n# tests/test_badf_build_activation.py\nstart = text.find(\"## badf-build → ACTIVE\"); self.assertGreater(start, 0, \"no BLD-E admission section\")\nend = text.find(\"\\n## \", start + 1); section = text[start:end if end > 0 else None]\nfor token in (...16 tokens...): self.assertIn(token, section, token)\n\n# tests/test_badf_git_activation.py — same shape, 15 tokens\n```\n\n**An inserted `## ` heading anywhere inside an admission section truncates it, and the token assertions fail.** **32** token assertions across the two modules sit behind heading depth.\n\n> **Counts corrected after filing (BADF-REV, verified independently here by AST).** The original body said 31, from a grep that quietly merged two loops with *different* scoping. Measured by walking the `For` nodes and reading each `assertIn`'s second argument:\n>\n> ```\n> build_activation:46    8 tokens -> assertIn(..., src)       NOT heading-coupled\n> build_activation:54   17 tokens -> assertIn(..., section)   coupled\n> git_activation:59     15 tokens -> assertIn(..., section)   coupled\n> ```\n>\n> So: **32 coupled** (17+15), not 31 — and separately, **8 of build's tokens assert against the whole source and cannot be broken by a heading at all.** The number is the part of a reader prices, and \"16 tokens in the section\" merged a coupled loop with an uncoupled one. Grep counts statements; the property is tuple length against a *specific target*, and only the AST distinguishes them.\n\nA third module asserts on the whole file, including a **negative**:\n\n```python\n# tests/test_badf_traceability.py:171-172\nself.assertIn(\"wp/WP-2026-NNNN-<slug>\", plane)\nself.assertNotIn(\"wp/BADF-WP-NNNN\", plane)     # breakable ONLY by adding text\n```\n\n## Measured, not reasoned — the tripwire fired\n\nMutation with a negative control, on `83f0472`:\n\n```\nNEGATIVE CONTROL (unmutated)                     GREEN  OK\nMUTANT ('## ' inserted inside the badf-build section)  RED  FAILED (failures=1)\nRESTORED                                         GREEN  OK\n```\n\n## Severity, corrected downward from how I first framed it\n\nThe phrasing that came up in review was *\"silently truncates.\"* **That is wrong and the correction matters.** The failure is **loud** — RED at test time, with the offending token named. What is invisible is the *edit-time* model: someone writing prose has no reason to treat heading depth as load-bearing, and nothing in the document says it is.\n\nSo this is **not** a vacuity bug — it is the good kind of guard, failing closed. It is a **latent-hazard/ergonomics** issue: the control is sound, the affordance is missing. Filing it at that severity rather than the more dramatic one.\n\n## Why it is worth filing anyway\n\nIn #278 I added 18 lines to this file and used `### ` rather than `## `. That was **correct by luck** — I chose the depth because it read better under the existing `##`, not because I knew 31 assertions depended on it. A 1-in-2 coin flip on an aesthetic judgement, in a file governance seats edit constantly. The next editor gets the same coin flip.\n\nCredit: BADF-REV raised it during the #278 review after I said the safety was mechanism-not-judgment; the mutation proof and the severity correction are mine.\n\n## Proposed shape (not a plan — no id claimed for a WP)\n\nCheapest first, per the minimality ladder:\n\n1. **A comment in the doctrine file itself**, at the top of each admission section: *\"section-scoped by the next `## `; inserting a `## ` heading inside this section breaks N token assertions in `tests/test_badf_<x>_activation.py`.\"* One line each, where the editor is actually looking.\n2. Optionally, a comment in the two tests naming the coupling from the other side.\n\n**Explicitly NOT proposed:** rewriting the section-scoping to be more robust. It works, it fails closed, and replacing a working control to improve an affordance is the over-engineering the Lean gate exists to prevent. The gap is that the document does not tell its editors what the tests assume.\n\n## Scope of the pattern — swept and closed (BADF-REV; re-verified here)\n\nI filed this saying I had *not* checked whether other docs share the coupling. That is now closed, and the answer is **no**:\n\n```\nmodules slicing a doc by \"## \" :  3\n  test_badf_build_activation.py     live scoping\n  test_badf_git_activation.py       live scoping\n  test_no_dead_assertions.py        FALSE POSITIVE\n```\n\nThe third is a `STRING_HASH = ''' ... '''` synthetic fixture — the dead-assertion detector's **own negative control** for a `#` inside a string literal, not live scoping code. Read before reporting, by both seats. `docs/00-operating-model.md`, the only other doc read by name, is used purely as a scratch file in the git tests (`write_text(\"stash me\")`).\n\n**So the pattern is exactly these two modules.** The issue does not understate it.\n\n*(There is a small recursion worth noting: that fixture exists because of an earlier instance of this same class — a naive `grep -nE '#.*self\\.assert'` false-positiving on this very file, where the `#` lives inside the string `\"## badf-build -> ACTIVE\"`. The detector was given a negative control precisely so it would not ship a false positive as its remedy.)*\n\n**Version tier:** patch (comments/docs only). **Review:** whoever takes it; not `contract_changed`, not `behavior_changed`.\n\n<!-- agent: architect -->\n\n",
+  "authorized_by": {
